window.__programmeIndexChunk("contexts",{"671f8cf47e4a2451b6fb307630bd5c6e4b0ff568e2b8190737bdb064d3c2b3d4":{"markdown":"<a id=\"l18-4\"><\/a>\n## 4. A central eigenunitary group is a normal Lebesgue coordinate\n\nLet \\(A\\ne0\\) be a von Neumann algebra, let \\(\\beta\\) be a point-ultraweakly continuous real action, and suppose \\(v_t\\in Z(A)\\) is a strongly continuous unitary group with\n\\[\n \\beta_s(v_t)=e^{ist}v_t.\n \\tag{L18.4.a}\n\\]\nWe prove that there is a unique faithful normal unital homomorphism\n\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n \\Pi:L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dr)\\longrightarrow Z(A),\\qquad \\Pi(e^{itr})=v_t,\\\\\n \\beta_s\\Pi(f)=\\Pi(a_sf),\\qquad a_sf(r)=f(r+s).\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{L18.4.b}\n\\]\nIts range is \\(v(\\mathbb R)''\\), and its inverse on that range is normal.\n\nTo obtain full normality, apply the already proved normal Haar-coordinate theorem [L31, Section 2](OA-FLOW-L31.md#l31-2) to \\(u_t=v_t\\) and the reversed action \\(\\gamma_s=\\beta_{-s}\\). Its negative-phase hypothesis is exact:\n\\(\\gamma_s(u_t)=e^{-ist}u_t\\).\nThat theorem constructs the coordinate through the faithful normal regular model and the full Fourier multiplication algebra, including its normal inverse. It gives \\(\\Pi(e^{itr})=v_t\\). Its covariance, or equality on characters followed by their ultraweak density and normality, gives the positive translation in (L18.4.b). Centrality follows because every generating unitary is central.\n\nIt is useful to see why this coordinate uses Lebesgue null sets. By [RF5](OA-FLOW-RF.md#oa-flow.rf.5), write \\(v_t=e^{itQ}\\), where \\(Q\\) is self-adjoint and affiliated with \\(Z(A)\\). Uniqueness of the generator gives\n\\[\n \\beta_s(Q)=Q+s1,\\qquad\n \\beta_s(E_Q(B))=E_Q(B-s)\n \\tag{L18.4.c}\n\\]\nfor every Borel set \\(B\\). For \\(\\omega\\in A_*^+\\), the finite scalar spectral measure \\(\\mu_\\omega(B)=\\omega(E_Q(B))\\) and the nonnegative interchange theorem [FF1](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.1) yield\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\omega(\\beta_s(E_Q(B)))\\,ds\n &=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\int_{\\mathbb R}1_B(r+s)\\,d\\mu_\\omega(r)\\,ds\\\\\n &=|B|\\,\\omega(1).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{L18.4.d}\n\\]\nIf \\(|B|=0\\), the continuous nonnegative function \\(s\\mapsto\\omega(\\beta_s(E_Q(B)))\\) has integral zero, so vanishes everywhere. At zero this gives \\(\\omega(E_Q(B))=0\\) for every positive normal functional, hence \\(E_Q(B)=0\\). Conversely, if \\(E_Q(B)=0\\), its translates are zero, and (L18.4.d) for any nonzero positive normal functional gives \\(|B|=0\\). This includes unbounded Borel sets and infinite integrals.\n\nNormal transport of the scalar spectral measure by \\(\\Pi\\) gives a projection-valued measure with imaginary powers \\(v_t\\). Generator uniqueness therefore identifies\n\\[\n \\Pi(1_B)=E_Q(B),\\qquad \\Pi(f)=f(Q)\n \\tag{L18.4.e}\n\\]\nfirst for Borel simple functions and then for all bounded Borel functions. Lebesgue measurable classes have Borel representatives, and the established null-set equivalence makes this independent of the representative. The normal coordinate construction proves preservation of arbitrary bounded increasing suprema; the null-set argument alone is not being used as a substitute for that normality assertion. Characters generate the full multiplication algebra in L31, so they also prove uniqueness of \\(\\Pi\\).\n\n","html":"<h2>4. A central eigenunitary group is a normal Lebesgue coordinate<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\ne0\\)\">\\(A\\ne0\\)<\/span> be a von Neumann algebra, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\)\">\\(\\beta\\)<\/span> be a point-ultraweakly continuous real action, and suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_t\\in Z(A)\\)\">\\(v_t\\in Z(A)\\)<\/span> is a strongly continuous unitary group with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\beta_s(v_t)=e^{ist}v_t.\n \\tag{L18.4.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\beta_s(v_t)=e^{ist}v_t.\n \\tag{L18.4.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nWe prove that there is a unique faithful normal unital homomorphism\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n \\Pi:L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dr)\\longrightarrow Z(A),\\qquad \\Pi(e^{itr})=v_t,\\\\\n \\beta_s\\Pi(f)=\\Pi(a_sf),\\qquad a_sf(r)=f(r+s).\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{L18.4.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n \\Pi:L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dr)\\longrightarrow Z(A),\\qquad \\Pi(e^{itr})=v_t,\\\\\n \\beta_s\\Pi(f)=\\Pi(a_sf),\\qquad a_sf(r)=f(r+s).\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{L18.4.b}\n\\]<\/span>\nIts range is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v(\\mathbb R)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(v(\\mathbb R)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>, and its inverse on that range is normal.<\/p>\n<p>To obtain full normality, apply the already proved normal Haar-coordinate theorem <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L31.html#l31-2\">L31, Section 2<\/a> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_t=v_t\\)\">\\(u_t=v_t\\)<\/span> and the reversed action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_s=\\beta_{-s}\\)\">\\(\\gamma_s=\\beta_{-s}\\)<\/span>. Its negative-phase hypothesis is exact:\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_s(u_t)=e^{-ist}u_t\\)\">\\(\\gamma_s(u_t)=e^{-ist}u_t\\)<\/span>.\nThat theorem constructs the coordinate through the faithful normal regular model and the full Fourier multiplication algebra, including its normal inverse. It gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Pi(e^{itr})=v_t\\)\">\\(\\Pi(e^{itr})=v_t\\)<\/span>. Its covariance, or equality on characters followed by their ultraweak density and normality, gives the positive translation in (L18.4.b). Centrality follows because every generating unitary is central.<\/p>\n<p>It is useful to see why this coordinate uses Lebesgue null sets. By <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-RF.html#oa-flow.rf.5\">RF5<\/a>, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_t=e^{itQ}\\)\">\\(v_t=e^{itQ}\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span> is self-adjoint and affiliated with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(A)\\)\">\\(Z(A)\\)<\/span>. Uniqueness of the generator gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\beta_s(Q)=Q+s1,\\qquad\n \\beta_s(E_Q(B))=E_Q(B-s)\n \\tag{L18.4.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\beta_s(Q)=Q+s1,\\qquad\n \\beta_s(E_Q(B))=E_Q(B-s)\n \\tag{L18.4.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nfor every Borel set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in A_*^+\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in A_*^+\\)<\/span>, the finite scalar spectral measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\omega(B)=\\omega(E_Q(B))\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\omega(B)=\\omega(E_Q(B))\\)<\/span> and the nonnegative interchange theorem <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.1\">FF1<\/a> yield\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\omega(\\beta_s(E_Q(B)))\\,ds\n &amp;=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\int_{\\mathbb R}1_B(r+s)\\,d\\mu_\\omega(r)\\,ds\\\\\n &amp;=|B|\\,\\omega(1).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{L18.4.d}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\omega(\\beta_s(E_Q(B)))\\,ds\n &amp;=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\int_{\\mathbb R}1_B(r+s)\\,d\\mu_\\omega(r)\\,ds\\\\\n &amp;=|B|\\,\\omega(1).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{L18.4.d}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|B|=0\\)\">\\(|B|=0\\)<\/span>, the continuous nonnegative function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\mapsto\\omega(\\beta_s(E_Q(B)))\\)\">\\(s\\mapsto\\omega(\\beta_s(E_Q(B)))\\)<\/span> has integral zero, so vanishes everywhere. At zero this gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega(E_Q(B))=0\\)\">\\(\\omega(E_Q(B))=0\\)<\/span> for every positive normal functional, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_Q(B)=0\\)\">\\(E_Q(B)=0\\)<\/span>. Conversely, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_Q(B)=0\\)\">\\(E_Q(B)=0\\)<\/span>, its translates are zero, and (L18.4.d) for any nonzero positive normal functional gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|B|=0\\)\">\\(|B|=0\\)<\/span>. This includes unbounded Borel sets and infinite integrals.<\/p>\n<p>Normal transport of the scalar spectral measure by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Pi\\)\">\\(\\Pi\\)<\/span> gives a projection-valued measure with imaginary powers <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_t\\)\">\\(v_t\\)<\/span>. Generator uniqueness therefore identifies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Pi(1_B)=E_Q(B),\\qquad \\Pi(f)=f(Q)\n \\tag{L18.4.e}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Pi(1_B)=E_Q(B),\\qquad \\Pi(f)=f(Q)\n \\tag{L18.4.e}\n\\]<\/span>\nfirst for Borel simple functions and then for all bounded Borel functions. Lebesgue measurable classes have Borel representatives, and the established null-set equivalence makes this independent of the representative. The normal coordinate construction proves preservation of arbitrary bounded increasing suprema; the null-set argument alone is not being used as a substitute for that normality assertion. Characters generate the full multiplication algebra in L31, so they also prove uniqueness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Pi\\)\">\\(\\Pi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L18.md","start_line":134,"end_line":179,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false},"67ac07068bff515dabcf60a9be7781975d7157103279f0f722ed69644f1e9c6a":{"markdown":"<a id=\"miv-countable-support\"><\/a>\n## 11. Countable support inside an arbitrary tensor product\n\nThe finite-leg construction is available for an arbitrary index set \\(I\\), including an uncountable one. At index \\(i\\), take a finite matrix factor and a faithful state as in (MIV8.a), with density \\(D_i\\). Use the pointed product representation and denote its product state by \\(\\varphi\\). For \\(J\\subset I\\), write \\(M_J\\) for the von Neumann subalgebra generated by the legs in \\(J\\), represented with the identity on the complementary tensor factor. The earlier programme [product construction and grouping theorem](../../OA-APPROX/infinite-tensor-products.html#theorem-5-1) prove\n\\[\n M=M_J\\ \\overline\\otimes\\ M_{I\\setminus J};\n\\]\nboth factors and the product state are faithful in the indicated representations. For \\(I=\\varnothing\\), the convention is \\(M=\\mathbb C\\).\n","html":"<h2>11. Countable support inside an arbitrary tensor product<\/h2>\n<p>The finite-leg construction is available for an arbitrary index set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>, including an uncountable one. At index <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span>, take a finite matrix factor and a faithful state as in (MIV8.a), with density <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_i\\)\">\\(D_i\\)<\/span>. Use the pointed product representation and denote its product state by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\subset I\\)\">\\(J\\subset I\\)<\/span>, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_J\\)\">\\(M_J\\)<\/span> for the von Neumann subalgebra generated by the legs in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span>, represented with the identity on the complementary tensor factor. The earlier programme <a href=\"../courses/OA-APPROX/infinite-tensor-products.html#theorem-5-1\">product construction and grouping theorem<\/a> prove\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n M=M_J\\ \\overline\\otimes\\ M_{I\\setminus J};\n\\]\">\\[\n M=M_J\\ \\overline\\otimes\\ M_{I\\setminus J};\n\\]<\/span>\nboth factors and the product state are faithful in the indicated representations. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(I=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>, the convention is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(M=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-MIV.md","start_line":966,"end_line":973,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false},"674885f6a41808b94bd870383901c5d88e1921326dcc09f3b9d882a01cb7be4f":{"markdown":"\\]\nAll factors are commuting central affiliated operators. Joint spectral cuts define their products; automorphisms preserve their spectral calculus. Consequently\n\\[\n 0<\\rho_n\\le\\lambda^n\\quad(n>0),\\qquad\n \\rho_{-m}\\ge\\lambda^{-m}\\quad(m>0).\n \\tag{LACC3}\n\\]\n\nThe complete GNS space is the orthogonal sum of the copies \\(u^nL^2(N,\\tau)\\). Indeed the coefficient expectation makes distinct degrees orthogonal, and\n\\(\\|\\Lambda_\\phi(u^nx)\\|^2=\\tau(x^*x)\\). To prove density for arbitrary \\(N\\), take the directed net of finite-trace projections \\(e\\uparrow1\\). For fixed \\(e\\), bounded strong-star polynomial approximation converges in the finite normal functional \\(a\\mapsto\\phi(eae)\\). The fixed right-module projections \\(J_\\phi eJ_\\phi\\) then converge strongly to one. This is the finite-corner argument of Section 2 with a net in place of its countable sequence, so no separability is introduced.\n\nOn the \\(n\\)-th summand, \\(\\Delta_\\phi^{it}\\) is multiplication by \\(\\rho_n^{it}\\). The spectral theorem therefore identifies its positive generator with multiplication by \\(\\rho_n\\). For positive \\(n\\) its spectrum lies in \\([0,\\lambda^n]\\); for negative \\(n=-m\\) it lies in \\([\\lambda^{-m},\\infty)\\). On the nonzero zero-degree summand it is the identity. Resolvent bounds uniform over the summands show\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\phi)\\cap(\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1})=\\{1\\}.\n \\tag{LAC3}\n\\]\nFor example, any closed subinterval of this open gap avoiding one has positive distance from the spectrum of every summand; their resolvents form a bounded direct sum. For explicit domain control, let \\(R_n\\) be positive self-adjoint multiplication by \\(\\rho_n\\) on \\(H_\\tau=L^2(N,\\tau)\\). Then\n\\[\n \\Delta_\\phi=\\bigoplus_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}R_n,\\qquad\n D(\\Delta_\\phi)=\\{(\\xi_n):\\xi_n\\in D(R_n),\\ \\sum_n\\|R_n\\xi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.\n \\tag{LACN3}\n\\]\nIts Borel functional calculus has the corresponding squared-sum domain with \\(R_n\\) replaced by \\(f(R_n)\\). The unitary powers agree on the complete finite GNS ideal, hence on the Hilbert sum; uniqueness of the spectral measures identifies their full domains. For real \\(a\\in(\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1})\\setminus\\{1\\}\\),\n\\[\n \\|(R_n-a)^{-1}\\|\\le\n \\bigl[\\min\\{a-\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1}-a,|1-a|\\}\\bigr]^{-1}.\n \\tag{LACN4}\n\\]\nThe direct sum inverse takes values in \\(D(\\Delta_\\phi)\\), because \\(R_n(R_n-a)^{-1}=1+a(R_n-a)^{-1}\\) is uniformly bounded. Both inverse identities follow on their full domains. This proves the full spectral gap; the nonzero degree-zero space supplies its eigenvalue one.\n\nThe fixed algebra is exactly \\(N\\). If \\(X\\) is modular invariant, applying \\(E_0(\\,\\cdot\\,u^{-n})\\) to its invariance gives\n\\[\n X_n\\theta^n(\\rho_n)^{it}=X_n\\quad\\text{for every }t.\n \\tag{LACC4}\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\ne0\\), (LACC3) puts the spectrum of \\(\\theta^n(\\rho_n)\\) strictly on one side of one. Its spectral calculus, tested against all characters \\(a^{it}\\), forces \\(X_n=0\\). More explicitly, an operator annihilating \\(a^{it}-1\\) for every rational \\(t\\) annihilates every spectral projection outside \\(\\{1\\}\\); this follows first for the joint scalar spectral measure and then for all vectors. There is no spectral mass at one. Formula (LACC1) now gives \\(X=E_0(X)\\in N\\). Conversely (LAC2) fixes \\(N\\). Thus \\(M_\\phi=N\\), and this dual weight has infinite multiplicity exactly when \\(N\\) is properly infinite.\n\n\n<a id=\"lac-diffuse\"><\/a>\n## 6. Every type III-zero centralizer has diffuse center\n\n### Diffuse center for every faithful semifinite weight\nThis consequence has no lacunarity, infinite-multiplicity or separability\nassumption. Let \\(\\phi\\) be any faithful n.s.f. weight on a type\n\\({\\rm III}_0\\) factor \\(M\\), and write \\(\\alpha=\\sigma^\\phi\\).\nSuppose \\(0\\neq e\\) were minimal in \\(Z(M_\\phi)\\).\nThe actual centralizer-corner theorem\n[OA-MOD CZ-09, (CZ.33)](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-CZ.html#justify-spectral-restriction-before-using-it)\ngives a faithful n.s.f. corner weight \\(\\phi_e\\), with\n\\(\\sigma^{\\phi_e}=\\alpha^e\\). In particular\n\n\\[\n (eMe)_{\\phi_e}=eM_\\phi e\\quad\\hbox{is a factor}.\n \\tag{LAC44}\n\\]\n\nThe fixed-algebra theorem of\n[GCC C15–16](OA-FLOW-GCC.md#gcc-intersection)\ngives \\(\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)=\\Gamma(\\alpha^e)\\).\nFactoriality is required of the fixed algebra in that theorem.\n\nHere the corner comparison must not assume \\(eMe\\cong M\\).\nThe [homogeneous matrix construction](OA-FLOW-DDP.md#dd-corners) gives an orthogonal filling family \\((p_i)_{i\\in I}\\) in \\(M\\), each equivalent to \\(e\\), with \\(v_i^*v_i=e\\), \\(v_iv_i^*=p_i\\). Proper infiniteness permits seeding with countably many copies, so \\(I\\) is infinite; choose an index \\(0\\in I\\). In \\(M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\\), [PC1](OA-FLOW-PC.md#pc-1) gives the strong sum\n\\[\n W=\\sum_{i\\in I}v_i\\otimes E_{0i},\\qquad\n W^*W=e\\otimes1=p,\\quad WW^*=1\\otimes E_{00}=q.\n \\tag{LACN6}\n\\]\n","html":"<p>]\nAll factors are commuting central affiliated operators. Joint spectral cuts define their products; automorphisms preserve their spectral calculus. Consequently\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n 0&lt;\\rho_n\\le\\lambda^n\\quad(n&gt;0),\\qquad\n \\rho_{-m}\\ge\\lambda^{-m}\\quad(m&gt;0).\n \\tag{LACC3}\n\\]\">\\[\n 0&lt;\\rho_n\\le\\lambda^n\\quad(n&gt;0),\\qquad\n \\rho_{-m}\\ge\\lambda^{-m}\\quad(m&gt;0).\n \\tag{LACC3}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The complete GNS space is the orthogonal sum of the copies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^nL^2(N,\\tau)\\)\">\\(u^nL^2(N,\\tau)\\)<\/span>. Indeed the coefficient expectation makes distinct degrees orthogonal, and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\Lambda_\\phi(u^nx)\\|^2=\\tau(x^*x)\\)\">\\(\\|\\Lambda_\\phi(u^nx)\\|^2=\\tau(x^*x)\\)<\/span>. To prove density for arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, take the directed net of finite-trace projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\uparrow1\\)\">\\(e\\uparrow1\\)<\/span>. For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span>, bounded strong-star polynomial approximation converges in the finite normal functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\mapsto\\phi(eae)\\)\">\\(a\\mapsto\\phi(eae)\\)<\/span>. The fixed right-module projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_\\phi eJ_\\phi\\)\">\\(J_\\phi eJ_\\phi\\)<\/span> then converge strongly to one. This is the finite-corner argument of Section 2 with a net in place of its countable sequence, so no separability is introduced.<\/p>\n<p>On the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>-th summand, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta_\\phi^{it}\\)\">\\(\\Delta_\\phi^{it}\\)<\/span> is multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_n^{it}\\)\">\\(\\rho_n^{it}\\)<\/span>. The spectral theorem therefore identifies its positive generator with multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_n\\)\">\\(\\rho_n\\)<\/span>. For positive <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> its spectrum lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,\\lambda^n]\\)\">\\([0,\\lambda^n]\\)<\/span>; for negative <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=-m\\)\">\\(n=-m\\)<\/span> it lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([\\lambda^{-m},\\infty)\\)\">\\([\\lambda^{-m},\\infty)\\)<\/span>. On the nonzero zero-degree summand it is the identity. Resolvent bounds uniform over the summands show\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\phi)\\cap(\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1})=\\{1\\}.\n \\tag{LAC3}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\phi)\\cap(\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1})=\\{1\\}.\n \\tag{LAC3}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor example, any closed subinterval of this open gap avoiding one has positive distance from the spectrum of every summand; their resolvents form a bounded direct sum. For explicit domain control, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_n\\)\">\\(R_n\\)<\/span> be positive self-adjoint multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_n\\)\">\\(\\rho_n\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_\\tau=L^2(N,\\tau)\\)\">\\(H_\\tau=L^2(N,\\tau)\\)<\/span>. Then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Delta_\\phi=\\bigoplus_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}R_n,\\qquad\n D(\\Delta_\\phi)=\\{(\\xi_n):\\xi_n\\in D(R_n),\\ \\sum_n\\|R_n\\xi_n\\|^2&lt;\\infty\\}.\n \\tag{LACN3}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Delta_\\phi=\\bigoplus_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}R_n,\\qquad\n D(\\Delta_\\phi)=\\{(\\xi_n):\\xi_n\\in D(R_n),\\ \\sum_n\\|R_n\\xi_n\\|^2&lt;\\infty\\}.\n \\tag{LACN3}\n\\]<\/span>\nIts Borel functional calculus has the corresponding squared-sum domain with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_n\\)\">\\(R_n\\)<\/span> replaced by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(R_n)\\)\">\\(f(R_n)\\)<\/span>. The unitary powers agree on the complete finite GNS ideal, hence on the Hilbert sum; uniqueness of the spectral measures identifies their full domains. For real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in(\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1})\\setminus\\{1\\}\\)\">\\(a\\in(\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1})\\setminus\\{1\\}\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|(R_n-a)^{-1}\\|\\le\n \\bigl[\\min\\{a-\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1}-a,|1-a|\\}\\bigr]^{-1}.\n \\tag{LACN4}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|(R_n-a)^{-1}\\|\\le\n \\bigl[\\min\\{a-\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1}-a,|1-a|\\}\\bigr]^{-1}.\n \\tag{LACN4}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe direct sum inverse takes values in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(\\Delta_\\phi)\\)\">\\(D(\\Delta_\\phi)\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_n(R_n-a)^{-1}=1+a(R_n-a)^{-1}\\)\">\\(R_n(R_n-a)^{-1}=1+a(R_n-a)^{-1}\\)<\/span> is uniformly bounded. Both inverse identities follow on their full domains. This proves the full spectral gap; the nonzero degree-zero space supplies its eigenvalue one.<\/p>\n<p>The fixed algebra is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> is modular invariant, applying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_0(\\,\\cdot\\,u^{-n})\\)\">\\(E_0(\\,\\cdot\\,u^{-n})\\)<\/span> to its invariance gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n X_n\\theta^n(\\rho_n)^{it}=X_n\\quad\\text{for every }t.\n \\tag{LACC4}\n\\]\">\\[\n X_n\\theta^n(\\rho_n)^{it}=X_n\\quad\\text{for every }t.\n \\tag{LACC4}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\ne0\\)\">\\(n\\ne0\\)<\/span>, (LACC3) puts the spectrum of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta^n(\\rho_n)\\)\">\\(\\theta^n(\\rho_n)\\)<\/span> strictly on one side of one. Its spectral calculus, tested against all characters <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a^{it}\\)\">\\(a^{it}\\)<\/span>, forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_n=0\\)\">\\(X_n=0\\)<\/span>. More explicitly, an operator annihilating <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a^{it}-1\\)\">\\(a^{it}-1\\)<\/span> for every rational <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> annihilates every spectral projection outside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{1\\}\\)\">\\(\\{1\\}\\)<\/span>; this follows first for the joint scalar spectral measure and then for all vectors. There is no spectral mass at one. Formula (LACC1) now gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X=E_0(X)\\in N\\)\">\\(X=E_0(X)\\in N\\)<\/span>. Conversely (LAC2) fixes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_\\phi=N\\)\">\\(M_\\phi=N\\)<\/span>, and this dual weight has infinite multiplicity exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is properly infinite.<\/p>\n<h2>6. Every type III-zero centralizer has diffuse center<\/h2>\n<h3>Diffuse center for every faithful semifinite weight<\/h3>\n<p>This consequence has no lacunarity, infinite-multiplicity or separability\nassumption. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span> be any faithful n.s.f. weight on a type\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\({\\rm III}_0\\)\">\\({\\rm III}_0\\)<\/span> factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, and write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha=\\sigma^\\phi\\)\">\\(\\alpha=\\sigma^\\phi\\)<\/span>.\nSuppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\neq e\\)\">\\(0\\neq e\\)<\/span> were minimal in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(M_\\phi)\\)\">\\(Z(M_\\phi)\\)<\/span>.\nThe actual centralizer-corner theorem\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-CZ.html#justify-spectral-restriction-before-using-it\">OA-MOD CZ-09, (CZ.33)<\/a>\ngives a faithful n.s.f. corner weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi_e\\)\">\\(\\phi_e\\)<\/span>, with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma^{\\phi_e}=\\alpha^e\\)\">\\(\\sigma^{\\phi_e}=\\alpha^e\\)<\/span>. In particular<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (eMe)_{\\phi_e}=eM_\\phi e\\quad\\hbox{is a factor}.\n \\tag{LAC44}\n\\]\">\\[\n (eMe)_{\\phi_e}=eM_\\phi e\\quad\\hbox{is a factor}.\n \\tag{LAC44}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The fixed-algebra theorem of\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GCC.html#gcc-intersection\">GCC C15–16<\/a>\ngives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)=\\Gamma(\\alpha^e)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)=\\Gamma(\\alpha^e)\\)<\/span>.\nFactoriality is required of the fixed algebra in that theorem.<\/p>\n<p>Here the corner comparison must not assume <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(eMe\\cong M\\)\">\\(eMe\\cong M\\)<\/span>.\nThe <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DDP.html#dd-corners\">homogeneous matrix construction<\/a> gives an orthogonal filling family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((p_i)_{i\\in I}\\)\">\\((p_i)_{i\\in I}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, each equivalent to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_i^*v_i=e\\)\">\\(v_i^*v_i=e\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_iv_i^*=p_i\\)\">\\(v_iv_i^*=p_i\\)<\/span>. Proper infiniteness permits seeding with countably many copies, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is infinite; choose an index <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\in I\\)\">\\(0\\in I\\)<\/span>. In <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\\)\">\\(M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#pc-1\">PC1<\/a> gives the strong sum\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n W=\\sum_{i\\in I}v_i\\otimes E_{0i},\\qquad\n W^*W=e\\otimes1=p,\\quad WW^*=1\\otimes E_{00}=q.\n \\tag{LACN6}\n\\]\">\\[\n W=\\sum_{i\\in I}v_i\\otimes E_{0i},\\qquad\n W^*W=e\\otimes1=p,\\quad WW^*=1\\otimes E_{00}=q.\n \\tag{LACN6}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-LAC.md","start_line":754,"end_line":821,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false},"6774b5de8e1413dfa909fe65019ed2831b7b50e951a9bc74482ca2f877ec7c33":{"markdown":"<a id=\"ps-obstruction\"><\/a>\n## 7. The full obstruction is a relative lifting class\n\nAll single automorphisms lift; choosing their lifts coherently is the issue. Form the pullback of the real covering of the center-rotation circle:\n\\[\n \\widehat G=\\{(\\alpha,r)\\in G\\times\\mathbb R:\n                       r+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha)\\},\n \\qquad\n 0\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow\\widehat G\n       \\longrightarrow G\\longrightarrow1,\n \\tag{PS30}\n\\]\nwhere \\(k\\mapsto(\\mathrm{id},kP)\\) and multiplication is\n\\((\\alpha,r)(\\beta,s)=(\\alpha\\beta,r+s)\\). The subgroup \\(K\\) has the prescribed splitting \\(\\alpha\\mapsto(\\alpha,0)\\).\n\nFor \\((\\alpha,r)\\in\\widehat G\\), the cocycle\n\\(b_{\\alpha,r}(t)=e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\) is periodic. The regular unitary of (PS27), with \\(d\\) replaced by \\(b\\), gives the entire normal automorphism\n\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r}(x)=\\alpha(x),\\qquad\n F_{\\alpha,r}(\\lambda_0(t))\n       =e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\lambda_0(t).\n \\tag{PS31}\n\\]\nIn particular\n\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r}F_{\\beta,s}=F_{\\alpha\\beta,r+s},\\qquad\n F_{\\alpha,r+kP}=\\gamma_{-k}F_{\\alpha,r}.\n \\tag{PS32}\n\\]\nThe signs in the second formula are forced by (PS11). These automorphisms commute with \\(\\gamma\\). They are continuous jointly in \\((\\alpha,r)\\), by the same compact-time estimates as before, with the added scalar exponential.\n\nThere are no additional compatible lifts hidden outside (PS31). Suppose first that a normal automorphism of the compact core extends \\(\\alpha\\) and commutes with \\(\\gamma\\). Compare it with one \\(F_{\\alpha,r}\\). Their quotient fixes \\(M\\) pointwise; its value on \\(\\lambda_0(t)\\), divided by \\(\\lambda_0(t)\\), lies in \\(M'\\cap C_{\\varphi,0}=\\mathbb C1\\). The group law makes this scalar a continuous character of \\(\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z\\), hence \\(e^{ikPt}\\) for a unique integer \\(k\\). (Lift the argument near zero and use the period, as for real characters.) The quotient is \\(\\gamma_{-k}\\), since the generators determine the whole normal map.\n\nThe same conclusion holds if a putative action is specified only as a continuous automorphism of the exact group square, with its prescribed actions on \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\) and the bottom groups. The right vertical isomorphism in (PS19) forces it to fix \\(\\delta_0\\). By (PS17) its value at \\(\\lambda_0(t)\\) is \\(v_t\\lambda_0(t)\\), \\(v_t\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\). Apply it to the conjugation relation with \\(x\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\). The resulting relation says that \\(v_t\\) and \\(d_\\alpha(t)\\) implement the same automorphism of the factor; their ratio is scalar. The group law then gives \\(v_t=e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\) for a real \\(r\\), and periodicity forces \\(r+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha)\\). Thus (PS31) exhausts continuous lifts of the whole square, too.\n\nFor completeness this parameter identification preserves topology, not just groups. Restriction from normal core automorphisms to \\(M\\) is u-continuous by extension of a normal functional to the ambient faithful representation. Locally choose continuous real representatives of \\(m(\\alpha)\\). After removing the corresponding continuously varying \\(F\\), only powers \\(\\gamma_k\\) remain. They form a discrete subgroup: the nonzero regular Fourier projection\n\\(p_0=T^{-1}\\int_0^T\\lambda_0(t)\\,dt\\)\nis sent by \\(\\gamma_k\\) to the mutually orthogonal projection \\(p_{-k}\\). If \\(\\gamma_{k_i}\\to\\mathrm{id}\\) in the u-topology, testing a unit vector in \\(p_0H\\) forces \\(k_i=0\\) eventually. For the group-square topology, compact-open convergence on the compact path \\(\\lambda_0(\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z)\\) gives the same result by integration. Hence continuous lift actions correspond exactly to continuous splittings of (PS30).\n\nHere is the explicit integer obstruction. Choose the Borel representative\n\\(r_0(\\alpha)\\in[0,P)\\) of \\(m(\\alpha)\\); it is zero on \\(K\\). Set\n\\[\n k(\\alpha,\\beta)\n =\\frac{r_0(\\alpha)+r_0(\\beta)-r_0(\\alpha\\beta)}P\\in\\mathbb Z.\n \\tag{PS33}\n\\]\nIt is a normalized Borel two-cocycle, because both sides of\n\\[\n k(\\alpha,\\beta)+k(\\alpha\\beta,\\eta)\n   =k(\\beta,\\eta)+k(\\alpha,\\beta\\eta)\n \\tag{PS34}\n\\]\nexpand to the same four real lifts. It vanishes whenever either entry is in \\(K\\), and depends only on their modulus classes. The chosen lifts satisfy the actual defect equation\n\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r_0(\\alpha)}F_{\\beta,r_0(\\beta)}\n   =\\gamma_{-k(\\alpha,\\beta)}\n      F_{\\alpha\\beta,r_0(\\alpha\\beta)}.\n \\tag{PS35}\n\\]\nReplacing \\(r_0\\) by \\(r_0+Pj\\), with integer-valued Borel \\(j\\) zero on \\(K\\), changes the defect to\n\\(k+j(\\alpha)+j(\\beta)-j(\\alpha\\beta)\\).\nThis proves independence of the relative class under every change of allowed lifts; it also specifies exactly what “relative” means here.\n\n**Extension criterion.** The given continuous \\(K\\times\\mathbb Z\\) action extends to a continuous \\(G\\times\\mathbb Z\\) action on the full square if and only if there is a continuous homomorphism\n\\[\n r:G\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad\n r(\\alpha)+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha),\\qquad r|_K=0.\n \\tag{PS36}\n\\]\nEquivalently, there is an integer-valued Borel \\(j\\), zero on \\(K\\), such that\n\\(k+\\partial j=0\\) and the real function \\(r_0+Pj\\) is continuous. Sufficiency is the action \\((\\alpha,n)\\mapsto F_{\\alpha,r(\\alpha)}\\gamma_n\\), using (PS32). Necessity follows from exhaustion and the group law in (PS32); the preceding topology argument gives continuity of \\(r\\). Restriction to the prescribed action gives \\(r|_K=0\\), since the \\(\\gamma_k\\)'s are distinct. For an abstract group action through normal compact-core automorphisms, omit continuity in the group parameter and the Borel requirement, while retaining normality of every individual automorphism and the same exact equations.\n\nThis is a usable criterion on the modulus alone. Give \\(Q=G/K\\) its quotient topology, identify it algebraically with the image of \\(m\\), and retain that quotient topology rather than silently substituting its circle subspace topology. Condition (PS36) says precisely that its injective homomorphism into \\(\\mathbb R/P\\mathbb Z\\) is the reduction of a continuous homomorphism \\(Q\\to\\mathbb R\\). In particular a nonzero torsion element in the modulus image prevents extension: if \\(m(\\alpha)\\ne0\\) has order \\(n\\), then \\(\\alpha^n\\in K\\), so (PS36) would give \\(nr(\\alpha)=r(\\alpha^n)=0\\), and therefore \\(m(\\alpha)=0\\), a contradiction. If the modulus is trivial the criterion is satisfied by \\(r=0\\). If the image is infinite cyclic generated by \\(q\\), has discrete quotient topology, and \\(a+P\\mathbb Z=q\\), the assignment \\(nq\\mapsto na\\) satisfies the criterion. These are positive and negative tests of the exact criterion, not a claim that every factor has one of these modulus images.\n\nAn absolute splitting of (PS30) alone is insufficient: it could send \\(\\alpha\\in K\\) to \\((\\alpha,n(\\alpha)P)\\), changing the prescribed action by \\(\\gamma_{-n(\\alpha)}\\). The relative condition in (PS36) is indispensable to the question as posed.\n\n![The real-cover carry and the resulting dual-automorphism defect](../assets/normalizer-characteristic-square/periodic-obstruction.png)\n\nThe three-step panel is the conditional torsion test \\(m(\\alpha)=-P/3\\): a lift \\(r=-P/3\\) accumulates \\(-P\\), so its third power differs from the prescribed lift of \\(\\alpha^3\\in K\\) by \\(\\gamma_1\\). No particular factor with that modulus image is asserted by the illustration.\n\nFinally, this obstruction is independent of the specified periodic weight. For another such weight \\(\\psi\\), use the actual compact comparison (PS15), with \\(b_t=e^{iat}[D\\varphi:D\\psi]_t\\). Ordered weight-cocycle covariance gives\n\\[\n d_\\alpha^\\varphi(t)b_t\n       =\\alpha(b_t)d_\\alpha^\\psi(t).\n \\tag{PS37}\n\\]\nThis is the exact normalized chain identity; the scalar \\(e^{iat}\\) occurs on both sides. Thus (PS15) intertwines \\(F_{\\alpha,r}^\\varphi\\) with \\(F_{\\alpha,r}^\\psi\\), with the same \\(r\\), and intertwines the dual actions. Changing the auxiliary \\(a\\) composes the comparison with a power of \\(\\gamma\\), which commutes with every \\(F\\). The central generators differ only by the scalar in (PS4), so the modulus parameter (PS24), the pullback extension, its prescribed \\(K\\)-splitting and the class (PS33) are unchanged.\n\n","html":"<h2>7. The full obstruction is a relative lifting class<\/h2>\n<p>All single automorphisms lift; choosing their lifts coherently is the issue. Form the pullback of the real covering of the center-rotation circle:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat G=\\{(\\alpha,r)\\in G\\times\\mathbb R:\n                       r+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha)\\},\n \\qquad\n 0\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow\\widehat G\n       \\longrightarrow G\\longrightarrow1,\n \\tag{PS30}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat G=\\{(\\alpha,r)\\in G\\times\\mathbb R:\n                       r+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha)\\},\n \\qquad\n 0\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow\\widehat G\n       \\longrightarrow G\\longrightarrow1,\n \\tag{PS30}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\mapsto(\\mathrm{id},kP)\\)\">\\(k\\mapsto(\\mathrm{id},kP)\\)<\/span> and multiplication is\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\alpha,r)(\\beta,s)=(\\alpha\\beta,r+s)\\)\">\\((\\alpha,r)(\\beta,s)=(\\alpha\\beta,r+s)\\)<\/span>. The subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> has the prescribed splitting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\mapsto(\\alpha,0)\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\mapsto(\\alpha,0)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\alpha,r)\\in\\widehat G\\)\">\\((\\alpha,r)\\in\\widehat G\\)<\/span>, the cocycle\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_{\\alpha,r}(t)=e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\)\">\\(b_{\\alpha,r}(t)=e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\)<\/span> is periodic. The regular unitary of (PS27), with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span> replaced by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, gives the entire normal automorphism\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r}(x)=\\alpha(x),\\qquad\n F_{\\alpha,r}(\\lambda_0(t))\n       =e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\lambda_0(t).\n \\tag{PS31}\n\\]\">\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r}(x)=\\alpha(x),\\qquad\n F_{\\alpha,r}(\\lambda_0(t))\n       =e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\lambda_0(t).\n \\tag{PS31}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn particular\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r}F_{\\beta,s}=F_{\\alpha\\beta,r+s},\\qquad\n F_{\\alpha,r+kP}=\\gamma_{-k}F_{\\alpha,r}.\n \\tag{PS32}\n\\]\">\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r}F_{\\beta,s}=F_{\\alpha\\beta,r+s},\\qquad\n F_{\\alpha,r+kP}=\\gamma_{-k}F_{\\alpha,r}.\n \\tag{PS32}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe signs in the second formula are forced by (PS11). These automorphisms commute with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span>. They are continuous jointly in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\alpha,r)\\)\">\\((\\alpha,r)\\)<\/span>, by the same compact-time estimates as before, with the added scalar exponential.<\/p>\n<p>There are no additional compatible lifts hidden outside (PS31). Suppose first that a normal automorphism of the compact core extends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> and commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span>. Compare it with one <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{\\alpha,r}\\)\">\\(F_{\\alpha,r}\\)<\/span>. Their quotient fixes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> pointwise; its value on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_0(t)\\)\">\\(\\lambda_0(t)\\)<\/span>, divided by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_0(t)\\)\">\\(\\lambda_0(t)\\)<\/span>, lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;\\cap C_{\\varphi,0}=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(M&#x27;\\cap C_{\\varphi,0}=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>. The group law makes this scalar a continuous character of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{ikPt}\\)\">\\(e^{ikPt}\\)<\/span> for a unique integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>. (Lift the argument near zero and use the period, as for real characters.) The quotient is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_{-k}\\)\">\\(\\gamma_{-k}\\)<\/span>, since the generators determine the whole normal map.<\/p>\n<p>The same conclusion holds if a putative action is specified only as a continuous automorphism of the exact group square, with its prescribed actions on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal U(M)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal U(M)\\)<\/span> and the bottom groups. The right vertical isomorphism in (PS19) forces it to fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_0\\)\">\\(\\delta_0\\)<\/span>. By (PS17) its value at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_0(t)\\)\">\\(\\lambda_0(t)\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_t\\lambda_0(t)\\)\">\\(v_t\\lambda_0(t)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_t\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)\">\\(v_t\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)<\/span>. Apply it to the conjugation relation with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)<\/span>. The resulting relation says that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_t\\)\">\\(v_t\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\alpha(t)\\)\">\\(d_\\alpha(t)\\)<\/span> implement the same automorphism of the factor; their ratio is scalar. The group law then gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_t=e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\)\">\\(v_t=e^{irt}d_\\alpha(t)\\)<\/span> for a real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, and periodicity forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha)\\)\">\\(r+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha)\\)<\/span>. Thus (PS31) exhausts continuous lifts of the whole square, too.<\/p>\n<p>For completeness this parameter identification preserves topology, not just groups. Restriction from normal core automorphisms to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is u-continuous by extension of a normal functional to the ambient faithful representation. Locally choose continuous real representatives of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m(\\alpha)\\)\">\\(m(\\alpha)\\)<\/span>. After removing the corresponding continuously varying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, only powers <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_k\\)\">\\(\\gamma_k\\)<\/span> remain. They form a discrete subgroup: the nonzero regular Fourier projection\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_0=T^{-1}\\int_0^T\\lambda_0(t)\\,dt\\)\">\\(p_0=T^{-1}\\int_0^T\\lambda_0(t)\\,dt\\)<\/span>\nis sent by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_k\\)\">\\(\\gamma_k\\)<\/span> to the mutually orthogonal projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{-k}\\)\">\\(p_{-k}\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_{k_i}\\to\\mathrm{id}\\)\">\\(\\gamma_{k_i}\\to\\mathrm{id}\\)<\/span> in the u-topology, testing a unit vector in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_0H\\)\">\\(p_0H\\)<\/span> forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k_i=0\\)\">\\(k_i=0\\)<\/span> eventually. For the group-square topology, compact-open convergence on the compact path <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_0(\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(\\lambda_0(\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span> gives the same result by integration. Hence continuous lift actions correspond exactly to continuous splittings of (PS30).<\/p>\n<p>Here is the explicit integer obstruction. Choose the Borel representative\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_0(\\alpha)\\in[0,P)\\)\">\\(r_0(\\alpha)\\in[0,P)\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m(\\alpha)\\)\">\\(m(\\alpha)\\)<\/span>; it is zero on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>. Set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n k(\\alpha,\\beta)\n =\\frac{r_0(\\alpha)+r_0(\\beta)-r_0(\\alpha\\beta)}P\\in\\mathbb Z.\n \\tag{PS33}\n\\]\">\\[\n k(\\alpha,\\beta)\n =\\frac{r_0(\\alpha)+r_0(\\beta)-r_0(\\alpha\\beta)}P\\in\\mathbb Z.\n \\tag{PS33}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt is a normalized Borel two-cocycle, because both sides of\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n k(\\alpha,\\beta)+k(\\alpha\\beta,\\eta)\n   =k(\\beta,\\eta)+k(\\alpha,\\beta\\eta)\n \\tag{PS34}\n\\]\">\\[\n k(\\alpha,\\beta)+k(\\alpha\\beta,\\eta)\n   =k(\\beta,\\eta)+k(\\alpha,\\beta\\eta)\n \\tag{PS34}\n\\]<\/span>\nexpand to the same four real lifts. It vanishes whenever either entry is in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, and depends only on their modulus classes. The chosen lifts satisfy the actual defect equation\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r_0(\\alpha)}F_{\\beta,r_0(\\beta)}\n   =\\gamma_{-k(\\alpha,\\beta)}\n      F_{\\alpha\\beta,r_0(\\alpha\\beta)}.\n \\tag{PS35}\n\\]\">\\[\n F_{\\alpha,r_0(\\alpha)}F_{\\beta,r_0(\\beta)}\n   =\\gamma_{-k(\\alpha,\\beta)}\n      F_{\\alpha\\beta,r_0(\\alpha\\beta)}.\n \\tag{PS35}\n\\]<\/span>\nReplacing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_0\\)\">\\(r_0\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_0+Pj\\)\">\\(r_0+Pj\\)<\/span>, with integer-valued Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span> zero on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, changes the defect to\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k+j(\\alpha)+j(\\beta)-j(\\alpha\\beta)\\)\">\\(k+j(\\alpha)+j(\\beta)-j(\\alpha\\beta)\\)<\/span>.\nThis proves independence of the relative class under every change of allowed lifts; it also specifies exactly what “relative” means here.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Extension criterion.<\/strong> The given continuous <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\times\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(K\\times\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span> action extends to a continuous <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\times\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(G\\times\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span> action on the full square if and only if there is a continuous homomorphism\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n r:G\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad\n r(\\alpha)+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha),\\qquad r|_K=0.\n \\tag{PS36}\n\\]\">\\[\n r:G\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad\n r(\\alpha)+P\\mathbb Z=m(\\alpha),\\qquad r|_K=0.\n \\tag{PS36}\n\\]<\/span>\nEquivalently, there is an integer-valued Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span>, zero on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, such that\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k+\\partial j=0\\)\">\\(k+\\partial j=0\\)<\/span> and the real function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_0+Pj\\)\">\\(r_0+Pj\\)<\/span> is continuous. Sufficiency is the action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\alpha,n)\\mapsto F_{\\alpha,r(\\alpha)}\\gamma_n\\)\">\\((\\alpha,n)\\mapsto F_{\\alpha,r(\\alpha)}\\gamma_n\\)<\/span>, using (PS32). Necessity follows from exhaustion and the group law in (PS32); the preceding topology argument gives continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>. Restriction to the prescribed action gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r|_K=0\\)\">\\(r|_K=0\\)<\/span>, since the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_k\\)\">\\(\\gamma_k\\)<\/span>'s are distinct. For an abstract group action through normal compact-core automorphisms, omit continuity in the group parameter and the Borel requirement, while retaining normality of every individual automorphism and the same exact equations.<\/p>\n<p>This is a usable criterion on the modulus alone. Give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q=G/K\\)\">\\(Q=G/K\\)<\/span> its quotient topology, identify it algebraically with the image of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>, and retain that quotient topology rather than silently substituting its circle subspace topology. Condition (PS36) says precisely that its injective homomorphism into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R/P\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R/P\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span> is the reduction of a continuous homomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\to\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(Q\\to\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>. In particular a nonzero torsion element in the modulus image prevents extension: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m(\\alpha)\\ne0\\)\">\\(m(\\alpha)\\ne0\\)<\/span> has order <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha^n\\in K\\)\">\\(\\alpha^n\\in K\\)<\/span>, so (PS36) would give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(nr(\\alpha)=r(\\alpha^n)=0\\)\">\\(nr(\\alpha)=r(\\alpha^n)=0\\)<\/span>, and therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m(\\alpha)=0\\)\">\\(m(\\alpha)=0\\)<\/span>, a contradiction. If the modulus is trivial the criterion is satisfied by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=0\\)\">\\(r=0\\)<\/span>. If the image is infinite cyclic generated by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span>, has discrete quotient topology, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a+P\\mathbb Z=q\\)\">\\(a+P\\mathbb Z=q\\)<\/span>, the assignment <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(nq\\mapsto na\\)\">\\(nq\\mapsto na\\)<\/span> satisfies the criterion. These are positive and negative tests of the exact criterion, not a claim that every factor has one of these modulus images.<\/p>\n<p>An absolute splitting of (PS30) alone is insufficient: it could send <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\in K\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\in K\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\alpha,n(\\alpha)P)\\)\">\\((\\alpha,n(\\alpha)P)\\)<\/span>, changing the prescribed action by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_{-n(\\alpha)}\\)\">\\(\\gamma_{-n(\\alpha)}\\)<\/span>. The relative condition in (PS36) is indispensable to the question as posed.<\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/normalizer-characteristic-square/periodic-obstruction.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/normalizer-characteristic-square/periodic-obstruction.png\" alt=\"The real-cover carry and the resulting dual-automorphism defect\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>The three-step panel is the conditional torsion test <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m(\\alpha)=-P/3\\)\">\\(m(\\alpha)=-P/3\\)<\/span>: a lift <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=-P/3\\)\">\\(r=-P/3\\)<\/span> accumulates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-P\\)\">\\(-P\\)<\/span>, so its third power differs from the prescribed lift of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha^3\\in K\\)\">\\(\\alpha^3\\in K\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_1\\)\">\\(\\gamma_1\\)<\/span>. No particular factor with that modulus image is asserted by the illustration.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, this obstruction is independent of the specified periodic weight. For another such weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span>, use the actual compact comparison (PS15), with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_t=e^{iat}[D\\varphi:D\\psi]_t\\)\">\\(b_t=e^{iat}[D\\varphi:D\\psi]_t\\)<\/span>. Ordered weight-cocycle covariance gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d_\\alpha^\\varphi(t)b_t\n       =\\alpha(b_t)d_\\alpha^\\psi(t).\n \\tag{PS37}\n\\]\">\\[\n d_\\alpha^\\varphi(t)b_t\n       =\\alpha(b_t)d_\\alpha^\\psi(t).\n \\tag{PS37}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is the exact normalized chain identity; the scalar <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{iat}\\)\">\\(e^{iat}\\)<\/span> occurs on both sides. Thus (PS15) intertwines <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{\\alpha,r}^\\varphi\\)\">\\(F_{\\alpha,r}^\\varphi\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{\\alpha,r}^\\psi\\)\">\\(F_{\\alpha,r}^\\psi\\)<\/span>, with the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, and intertwines the dual actions. Changing the auxiliary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> composes the comparison with a power of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span>, which commutes with every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>. The central generators differ only by the scalar in (PS4), so the modulus parameter (PS24), the pullback extension, its prescribed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>-splitting and the class (PS33) are unchanged.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-PSQ.md","start_line":318,"end_line":405,"recorded_range_hash_matched":true}});
